    },
    {
      "id": "blk-part04-4-definite-iteration-0022",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 22,
      "source_hash": "6ecc2b7c5d11db3acb773d0fcae060ba82781498e2bc76e4929316249b01c132",
      "translation_status": "pending",
      "original": {
        "language": "en",
        "content": "Often you know how many times you want to repeat a certain bit of code. You might, for example, wish to go through all the numbers between 1 and 100. The `range` function plugged into a `for` loop will do this for you."
      },
      "translation": {
        "language": "vi",
        "content": ""
      },
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "id": "blk-part04-4-definite-iteration-0023",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 23,
      "source_hash": "7baa84ff25f740e26fc0b9d873ecf80791ead0734a1f0462aab756f6a93c1665",
      "translation_status": "pending",
      "original": {
        "language": "en",
        "content": "There are a few different ways to call the range function. The simplest way is to give the function just one argument, which signifies the end-point of the range. The end-point itself is excluded, just like with string slices. In other words, the function call `range(n)` provides a loop with a range from 0 to `n-1`:"
      },
      "translation": {
        "language": "vi",
        "content": ""
      },
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
